issue.body:
Consider the following code

```
        var x = "hello";

        setTimeout(() => {
            x = "world!";
        }, 100);

        $scope.$watch(() => x, () => {
            console.log("Got x == " + x + " in zone " + window.zone.$id);
        });
```

I would expect that the watch would always be called inside ngZone. However, after fixing #6385, the above watch will report being run in zone 1 and thus breaking further change detection.

Changing upgade_adapter back to ngZone.run(() => rootScope.$apply()) fixes the issue.
